          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            An
            %strong abstract clone
            is a structure that describes a single-sorted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+theory"} algebraic theory
            in a presentation-invariant way.
          %p
            An abstract clone is equivalent to a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+operad"} cartesian operad
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+multicategory"} cartesian multicategory
            with one object. However, clones are presented differently, and quite economically, with projections rather than with symmetries, contraction and weakening.
          %p
            Abstract clones are equivalent to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theories"} Lawvere theories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitary+monads"} finitary monads
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A set of algebraic operations on a fixed set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is a
            %strong concrete clone
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            if it contains all (component) projections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n}\to S
            and is closed under composition (“superposition”).
          %p
            An
            %strong abstract clone
            consists of an abstract set of “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            \-ary operations” for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            together with projection and composition operations. This is the notion that’s equivalent to a cartesian operad or a Lawvere theory.
          %p More precisely, the usual presentation of abstract clone comprises
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
                for each natural number
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p
                functions
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi η
                          %mi n
                        %mo :
                        %mi n
                        %mo →
                        %mi T
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_n : n \to T(n)
            %li
              %p
                functions
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mrow
                          %mi m
                          %mo ,
                          %mi n
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi m
                      %mo →
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{m,n} : T(m) \times T(n)^m\to T(n)
          %p such that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi j
                      %mo .
                      %mi t
                      %mo =
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta(i) \rhd j. t=t
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi i
                      %mo .
                      %mi η
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \rhd i.\eta(i) =t
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi i
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi j
                      %mo .
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi i
                      %mo .
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi j
                      %mo .
                      %mi v
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \rhd i.(u(i) \rhd j.v(j)) = (t \rhd i.u(i)) \rhd j. v(j)
          %p
            where we omit subscripts and write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo ⊳
                  %mi i
                  %mo .
                  %mi u
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t \rhd i.u(i))
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi u
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c(t,(u_i)_i)
          %p
            This resembles the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29#RelationToMonadsInCategoryTheory"} Kleisli triple presentation
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is a natural number rather than an arbitrary set; in this sense it is the Kleisli triple form of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+monad"} relative monad
            for the embedding
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi FinSet
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{FinSet}\to\mathbf{Set}
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#concrete_clones_as_abstract_clones Concrete clones as abstract clones
          %p
            Suppose a concrete clone is given on a set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n\to S
            of different arities, closed under composition and containing all the projections. We regard this as an abstract clone by putting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
            as the set of admitted operations
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n\to S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_n
            pick out the projections, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{m,n}
            is multi-ary function composition.
          %h3#abstract_clones_from_algebraic_signatures Abstract clones from algebraic signatures
          %p
            An
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/signature+%28in+logic%29"} algebraic signature
            comprises, for each number
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Σ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_n
            of formal
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_0=\{1\}
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_1=\{(-)^{-1}\}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_2=\{\cdot\}
          %p
            We then build for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            the terms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            variables, written
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mi …
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⊢
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1\dots x_n\vdash x_i
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 1
                      %mi …
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1\dots x_n\vdash x_i
                is a term if
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi i
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1\leq i\leq n
            %li
              %p
                if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 1
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi …
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi n
                      %mo ⊢
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\vdash t_1\ \ \dots\ \  n\vdash t_m
                are terms and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi Σ
                        %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma\in \Sigma_m
                then
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi σ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi m
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\vdash \sigma(t_1,\dots, t_m)
                is a term. Here we write
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mn 1
                        %mi …
                        %msub
                          %mi x
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n =(x_1\dots x_n)
          %p
            Now we form an abstract clone by putting
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi t
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi t
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext is a term
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_\Sigma(n)=\{t\ \ |\ \ (n\vdash t\ \ \text{ is a term})\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_n
            picks out the variables, and the function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{m,n}
            amount to substitutions of terms for variables, which produces well-formed terms.
          %p
            The inhabitants of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi T
                    %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_\Sigma
            are sometimes called “derived operations”, since although they are not in the signature they have an interpretation in any model.
          %h3#abstract_clones_from_presentations_of_algebraic_theories Abstract clones from presentations of algebraic theories
          %p
            An algebraic theory over a signature is given by a collection of axioms of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi t
                    %mo =
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma\vdash t=u
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash t
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash u
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∼
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim_n
            of terms by closing under substitution instances, congruence, and reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity. These are the equations that are true in all models of the theory.
          %p
            We can now form an abstract clone for the theory, derived from the abstract clone for the signature. Put
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mi Σ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∼
                          %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)=T_\Sigma(n)/_{\sim_n}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            respect equivalence relations because we closed under substitution instances and congruence.
          %p
            For example, starting from the theory of groups,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)
            will be the free group on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            generators.
          %h3#algebraic_theories_from_abstract_clones Algebraic theories from abstract clones
          %p
            Conversely, any abstract clone can be regarded as a presentation of an algebraic theory. The signature has
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Σ
                      %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma_n=T(n)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_n(i) = x_i
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 1
                      %mi …
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi s
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi i
                      %mo .
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(t_1\dots t_n) = (s \rhd i. t(i))
                \.
          %p An abstract clone is a presentation of a “saturated” algebraic theory: all derived operations are equivalent to basic operations that are already in the signature.
          %h3#lawvere_theories_and_abstract_clones Lawvere theories and abstract clones
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is an abstract clone, we can form a category whose objects are natural numbers and where a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo →
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m\to n
            is a tuple
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(m)^n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊳
                    %mi j
                    %mo .
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (g\circ f)(i)=(g(i)\rhd j.f(j))
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
          %p
            (This is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+category"} opposite category
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleisli+category"} Kleisli category
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            regarded as a
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+monad"} relative monad
          %p
            Conversely, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is a Lawvere theory then we can build a clone
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)=L(n,1)
          %h2#algebras_of_abstract_clones_and_connection_with_finitary_monads Algebras of abstract clones and connection with finitary monads
          %p
            We can define an
            %strong
              algebra of an abstract clone
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            to be a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            together with
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ⊳
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \rhd : X^n\to X
            for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in T(n)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi η
                        %mi i
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi i
                      %mo .
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_i \rhd i.x(i)= x(i)
                ;
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi i
                      %mo .
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi j
                      %mo .
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi t
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi i
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊳
                      %mi j
                      %mo .
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi j
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (t\rhd i.u(i))\rhd j.x(j)= t\rhd i.(u(i)\rhd j.x(j))
                \.
          %p
            Here we are writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊳
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rhd
            for both the clone composition and the algebra structure, and again writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ⊳
                  %mi i
                  %mo .
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \rhd i.x(i)
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⊳
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\rhd(x_i)_i
          %p For example, an algebra for the abstract clone of groups is a group.
          %p
            Now the category of
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi Alg
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T\mathbf{-Alg} \to \mathbf{Set}
          %p
            One can show that this functor is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic"} monadic
            by showing it preserves limits, satisfies the solution set condition, reflects isomorphisms, and has reflexive coequalizers. Thus every abstract clone gives rise to a monad.
          %p
            Another way to define this monad is to note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            can be regarded as a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi FinSet
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{FinSet}\to \mathbf{Set}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(f):T(m)\to T(n)
            given by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo ⊳
                    %mi i
                    %mo .
                    %mi η
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(f)(t)=(t\rhd i.\eta(f(i)))
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M:\mathbf{Set}\to\mathbf{Set}
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            or by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi n
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lan
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(X) = \int^n T(n)\times X^n = (\mathrm{Lan}\,T)(X)
          %p
            In other words, an inhabitant of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M(X)
            is a derived operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t\in T(n)
            formally applied to an
            = succeed "-tuple" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            can be given the structure of a monad on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Set}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Moore+algebras"} Eilenberg-Moore algebras
            are the
            = succeed "-algebras." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            Conversely, suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            on the category of sets. We can define an abstract clone by regarding a natural number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            as a finite set and putting
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(n)=M(n)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            is the unit of the monad and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is the Kleisli composition (called “monadic bind”).
          %p
            This gives an equivalence between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finitary+monads"} finitary monads
            and abstract clones. They are also the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+with+arities"} monads with arities
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi FinSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{FinSet}
          %h2#monadicity_of_the_category_of_abstract_clones Monadicity of the category of abstract clones
          %p The definition of abstract clones given here is itself a presentation of algebraic structure. Indeed we can consider an obvious notion of homomorphism of clones, and then the forgetful functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi AbstractClones
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi FinSet
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{AbstractClones} \to [\mathbf{FinSet},\mathbf{Set}]
          %p
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monadic"} monadic
          %p
            In fact this adjunction is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched"} enriched
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi FinSet
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathbf{FinSet},\mathbf{Set}]
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+category"} locally presentable category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            are replaced by finitely presentable objects. Then the plain abstract clones themselves are algebras for an abstract clone enriched in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi FinSet
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathbf{FinSet},\mathbf{Set}]
          %p
            Another view of this adjunction starts from viewing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi FinSet
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathbf{FinSet},\mathbf{Set}]
            as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi FinSet
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{FinSet}\to \mathbf{Set}
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimit"} filtered colimit
            preserving endofunctors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Set}\to\mathbf{Set}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi FinSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{FinSet}(1,-)
            and tensor product is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi m
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi m
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Lan
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F \otimes G) (n) = \int^m F(m) \times G(n)^m = ((\mathrm{Lan}\,F)\circ G)(n)
          %p
            Then the category of abstract clones is (equivalent to) the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            for this monoidal structure. Since a finitary monad is a filtered-colimit-preserving “monoid in the category of endofunctors”, this also gives an abstract view of the connection between abstract clones and finitary monads on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Set}
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              Ágnes Szendrei,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Clones in universal algebra
          %p A rather general framework is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Zhaohua Luo,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Clone theory, its syntax and semantics, applications to universal algebra, lambda calculus and algebraic logic
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0810.3162"} arxiv/0810.3162
            %li
              Dietlinde Lau,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Function algebras on finite sets: Basic course on many-valued logic and clone theory
          %p A common generalization of a clone and of an operad is proposed, using a new notion of a verbal category, in
          %ul
            %li
              S. Tronin,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abstract clones and operads
              %a{:href => "http://rd.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1023%2FA%3A1016340806503.pdf"} link
          %p Another unification of clones and operads is via the formalism in
          %ul
            %li
              Pierre-Louis Curien,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Operads, clones, and distributive laws
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.3050"} arxiv/1205.3050
          %p See also the thesis
          %ul
            %li
              Miles Gould,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Coherence for operadic theories
              %a{:href => "http://theses.gla.ac.uk/689/1/2009gouldphd.pdf"} pdf
          %p Algebraic theories are treated as abstract clones in the enriched setting (but abstractly), and monadicity is dealt with, in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"}> Max Kelly
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Power"} John Power
              = succeed "." do
                %em Adjunctions whose counits are coequalizers, and presentations of finitary enriched monads
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-4049%2893%2990092-8"} doi:10.1016/0022-4049(93)90092-8
          %p
            Substitution algebras as a theory over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi FinSet
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Set
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\mathbf{FinSet},\mathbf{Set}]
            are presented in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcelo+Fiore"}> Marcelo Fiore
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gordon+Plotkin"} Gordon Plotkin
              and Daniele Turi. Abstract syntax and variable binding. Proc LICS 1999.
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://www.dcs.ed.ac.uk/home/dt/FiorePlotkinTuri.pdf"} preprint
          %p One description of enriched abstract clones is in Section 5 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sam+Staton"}> Sam Staton
              , An algebraic presentation of predicate logic. FOSSACS 2013.
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/samuel.staton/papers/fossacs13.pdf"} preprint
        .revisedby
